<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chao Cuo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 200–154 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chao Cuo was a Chinese philosopher, politician and writer who served as an adviser and official under Emperor Wen and Emperor Jing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>. In a memorandum of 169 BCE he compared Han and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> tactics and proposed settling government-supported migrants in walled towns on the northern frontier, to farm and train as militia; Emperor Wen approved it. He helped revive the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-documents">Classic of History</a>, taught by the daughter of the aged scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fu-sheng">Fu Sheng</a>, though later Eastern Han scholars called him a Legalist. Made Imperial Secretary in 155 BCE, he urged cutting down the princely kingdoms, and when seven kings rebelled his rivals persuaded Emperor Jing to have him executed in 154 BCE.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Chao Cuo was born in Yuzhou, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a>. While a junior official in the Ministry of Ceremonies he was sent by Emperor Wen to study with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fu-sheng">Fu Sheng</a>, an academician of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin dynasty</a> who had hidden and partly recovered a copy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-documents">Classic of History</a> during the Qin purge of literature. His appointment in 155 BCE as Imperial Secretary in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> made him one of the three most senior officials of the central government, known for his knowledge of warfare, agriculture, economics and frontier management.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His thinking drew on Legalist writers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shang-yang">Shang Yang</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shen-buhai">Shen Buhai</a>, and the essays of his preserved in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-han">Book of Han</a> show no influence of Confucian ethics. He was among the first ministers to urge Emperor Wen to build up cavalry to match the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>, and advised using surrendered Xiongnu horsemen in Han armies. Like Jia Yi he argued for ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heqin">heqin</a> marriage and tribute treaty, but on practical grounds, since the Xiongnu had broken it again and again; it was abandoned only after the Battle of Mayi in 133 BCE.</p><p>In his memorandum Guard the Frontiers and Protect the Borders he judged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> better on rough ground and at mounted archery, but weaker on level plains against Han iron armour, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crossbow">crossbows</a> and infantry. His frontier towns were to hold at least a thousand households, with double walls 150 paces apart and sandy fields to show intruders' footprints, settled first by convicts, then by slaves and volunteers, with houses, tools, food and rewards. The plan influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a>'s colonisation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu-corridor">Hexi Corridor</a>. He also wrote of peasant families driven by sudden taxes to borrow at two hundred per cent interest and sell their land to merchants.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Chao Cuo helped lead the court's efforts to reduce the subordinate kingdoms. When the kings of seven kingdoms plotted rebellion, his enemies at court, among them Yuan Ang, persuaded Emperor Jing that his death would appease them. After his execution the emperor soon saw that the claims were false, as Liu Bi, King of Wu, and his allies rose in open revolt. The historian Nishijima Sadao later cited Chao's account of farmers and merchants as evidence of the contrast between their lives in the Former Han.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chao Cuo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Western Han most were small owner-cultivators, ranked above tenants and hired labourers: by the official <b>Chao Cuo</b>’s reckoning a family of five farmed no more than about 100 mu, some 11 acres.</blockquote><a href="/societies/han-china">Social Hierarchy of Han China</a></li></ul></section>
